===Entering League of Legends (2018-2020)===
Hanwha Life Esports entered League of Legends esports by acquiring [[ROX Tigers]]' league spot and line-up ahead of the [[LCK/2018/Summer|2018 LCK Summer Split]]. <ref name="Hanwha acquires ROX">{{cite web|url=https://www.facebook.com/TigersLOL/?ref=br_rs|title=ROX Tigers acquired by Hanwha Life Esports|author=@TigersLoL|date=2018-04-16}}</ref> They made their debut in a 1-2 loss against [[Griffin]], before eventually failing to qualify for playoffs with a 10/-/8 record in the regular season. [[SoHwan]], [[Bono]], [[Tempt]], [[Lava]], [[Sangyoon]] and [[Key]] would be the roster representing the team in the [[KeSPA_Cup/2018|2018 KeSPA Cup]], where they lost against '''KeG Seoul''', a roster consisting of [[Doran]], [[Ellim]], [[Plex]], [[Gumayusi]] and [[POP]]. This resulted in a first round exit, placing 13-19th.

For the [[LCK/2019/Spring|2019 Spring Season]], Hanwha Life Esports signed [[Thal]] as a new toplaner, replacing [[SoHwan]]. They would remain as a middle-of-the-pack team, placing 6th in the regular season and once again narrowly missing out on playoffs, this time with a 9/-/9 record. In the [[LCK/2019/Summer|2019 Summer Season]], [[Moojin]] moved to the starting roster over [[Bono]], which resulted in the team placing {{Ordinal|9}} in the regular season. This meant that Hanwha Life Esports had to attend the relegation tournament, where they qualified for [[LCK/2020/Spring|The 2020 LCK Spring Season]] by beating both [[Team Dynamics]] and [[Jin Air Green Wings]] in the lower bracket. Hanwha Life Esports rebuilt the team ahead of the [[KeSPA Cup/2019|2019 KeSPA Cup]], with [[CuVee]], [[Haru]], [[Zenit]] and [[Lehends]] joining the team. They qualified for the playoffs round by beating [[ESports Connected]] and [[KT Rolster]], but lost in the quarterfinals with a 2-1 loss against champions [[Afreeca Freecs]]. For the 2020 season, [[Lava]] returned to the starting roster to replace [[Zenit]]. Despite their promising performance at the KeSPA Cup, this team ended the regular season in 8th place with a 6/-/12 record. In the [[LCK/2020/Summer|2020 Summer Season]], [[Viper (Korean player)|Viper]] joined the team as the new AD Carry. This line-up only managed to win 2 games in the entire season, placing 9th ahead of only [[SeolHaeOne Prince]].

===Debuting on the International Stage (2021)===
Ahead of 2021, it was announced that the '''LCK''' would move into a franchised league format, with Hanwha Life Esports being 1 of 10 teams to be selected. Ahead of the regular season, the [[KeSPA Cup/2020|2020 KeSPA Cup]] allowed the team to show their new line-up for the first time, consisting of [[Morgan]], [[Arthur (Park Mi-reu)|Arthur]], [[Chovy]], [[Deft]] and [[Vsta]]. The team qualified for playoffs after going 2/-/2 in the group stage, and beat [[Gen.G Esports]] in the round of 6. In the semifinals, they lost against champions [[DAMWON Gaming]]. This roster exceeded expectation in [[LCK/2021/Spring|Spring]], placing {{Ordinal|3}} in the regular season ahead of [[T1]]. With the roster staying the same ahead of [[LCK/2021/Summer|Summer]], [[Arthur (Park Mi-reu)|Arthur]] was replaced by [[Willer]] halfway through the season. Hanwha Life Esports placed 8th in the summer season, with a 7/-/11 record. Despite this, they qualified for the [[LCK/2021/Regional_Finals|Regional Finals]] due to their placement in spring, and with victories against [[SANDBOX Gaming]] and [[Nongshim RedForce]], Hanwha Life Esports managed to qualify for [[World Championship/2021|Worlds 2021]]. After only losing against [[LNG Esports]] in play-ins, they secured a spot in the group stage where they placed second following a tiebreaker loss against [[Royal Never Give Up]]. In the quarter finals, Hanwha Life Esports lost 3-0 against [[T1]], resulting in a {{Ordinal|5-8}} place finish in their first ever international tournament.

