==History==
===Formation and entrance into the LFL (2020-2021)===
Known as ''Kameto Corp'' at the beginning, the organisation was formed by the French popular streamer [https://www.twitch.tv/kamet0 Kameto]. In May 2020, Kameto Corp announced their entrance into League of Legends by acquiring the slot and the players of [[Unfazed Esport]] in [[LFL_Division_2/2020|Division 2]], the second division of [[LFL]]. Their first team is composed of Adam "[[Adam]]" Maanane, Philippe "[[Akabane]]" Le, Ilias "[[Nuclearint]]" Bizriken, Julien "[[Jujutw0]]" Izzo, and Jérémy "[[Helaz]]" François with [[Rejakar]], [[Galdix]], [[Striker]], and [[Reha]] as the coaching staff. The team quickly gained a large following due to the popularity of Kameto, who streamed the matches of the team on his channel every week. 

Despite a good first split with a third place with 12-6 records, the objective to promotion in [[LFL]] is not reached and it's announced that the contracts of the players will be not extended for the next year. 

Kameto Corp later announced that [https://twitter.com/PrimeTimeFUT Prime] joined the project as a Co-Owner. At this point the team also rebranded to ''Karmine Corp''. Their League of Legends roster was promoted to the first division of the [[LFL/2021/Spring|LFL]] the following year as they acquired the vacant slot left by [[Team Oplon]]. Karmine Corp followed by rebuilding their new team around their old top laner [[Adam]]. Completing their line-up by signing Jakub "[[Cinkrof]]" Rokicki, Lucas "[[Saken]]" Fayard, Matthew "[[xMatty]]" Coombs, and Raphaël "[[Targamas]]" Crabbé. 

At the beginning of the 2021 [[LFL/2021/Spring|LFL Spring Split]], Karmine Corp wasn't considered a favorite for the title. But managed to consistently find victories throughout the regular season, keeping the second place throughout. During this split, Karmine Corp formed a rivalry against [[Solary]] due to the relationship between Kameto and [[Solary]]'s CEO, Sakor "[[LRB]]" Ros, who also is a famous French streamer with a large following. 

In the last week of the split, [[Team BDS|BDS]] fell out of the race for first place, leaving Karmine Corp and [[Misfits Premier]] tied for first place. Karmine Corp managed to win the tiebreaker match and finished first in the regular season, qualifying them for the Grand Finals of the Playoffs. Also guaranteeing them a slot in [[European Masters/2021/Spring|European Masters]]. As the bracket progressed Karmine Corp once again found themselves facing [[Misfits Premier]], this time convincingly beating them in a 3-0 series. Winning their first [[LFL]] title in their very first season.

After winning the [[LFL]] Spring Split, Karmine Corp was considered a favourite at [[European Masters/2021/Spring|EU Masters]]. As the first seed of the LFL Karmine Corp was seeded directly into the Group Stages, where they were placed in Group D against [[FC Schalke 04 Evolution]], [[Cream Real Betis]], and [[JD-XL|BT Excel]]. The team there managed to go undefeated in the group stage, ending on a 6-0 record to qualify themselves for the playoffs. Within the playoffs, they made their way through the bracket as they beat [[Suppup eSports]] and [[UCAM Esports Club]] both in 2-1 series. In the Grand Finals, they'd again face off against [[BT Excel]] which they managed to beat 3-1 to claim the European Masters Title. The match also became the most viewed [[LEC|EU Masters]]'s final with a peak of 377,531 viewers, breaking the previous record from [[European Masters/2020/Spring|Spring in 2020]]. <ref>{{cite web |url=https://escharts.com/tournaments/lol/european-masters-spring-2021|title=European Masters Spring 2021 / Statistics|date=2021-05-02 |publisher=Esports Charts|language={{Flag|usuk}} English}}</ref>

For the following [[LFL/2021/Summer|Summer]] split, Karmine Corp brought in Lucas "[[Cabochard]]" Simon-Meslet as [[Adam]] left to join [[Fnatic]]. This small change didn't change the team, as in the regular season Karmine Corp once again found themselves in first place with a 14-4 record. However this time in the Grand Finals they lost 1-3 against [[Misfits Premier]]. Still with their second-place finish they qualified for their second [[European_Masters/2021/Summer|European Masters]].

Again placed in Group D, Karmine Corp had to face off against [[Giants]], [[mousesports]], and [[Macko Esports]]. Again they had a dominant showing in groups, only dropping a single game against Giants to end on a 5-1 record. Heading into the Knockout Stage, Karmine Corp made swift work of [[UCAM Esports Club]] as they swept the Spanish team 3-0. In the following semifinals against [[BIG]], however, they had more work but came out on top in a back-and-forth best of five to qualify for the Finals. Facing off against [[Fnatic Rising]] they initially found themselves up 2-0 in the series, but Fnatic was able to tie up the series with the following two games. But the fifth game was settled in just above 20 minutes as Karmine Corp came out victorious and secured their second European Masters title.

===Continuing the success in European Masters (2022-2023)===
Heading into the 2022 year, Karmine Corp had to rebuild their team, as [[Cinkrof]], [[xMatty]], and [[Targamas]] left the team to join various teams in the [[LEC]]. To fill the void left in the team Karmine Corp signed Doğukan "[[113]]" Balcı, Martin "[[Rekkles]]" Larsson, and Jules "[[Hantera]]" Bourgeois. The team went off to a good start in the regular season off the [[LFL/2022/Spring|2022 Spring]] split, finishing in second in the regular season, beating [[Team BDS Academy]] in the tiebreaker for second to secure themselves advanced seeding into the Winners's Finals. However here they were swept by [[LDLC OL]] and sent into Losers' Finals. The team was unable to come back from their loss as they were also beaten by [[Team BDS Academy]]. Nonetheless, the team had secured a spot at [[European_Masters/2022/Spring|EU Masters]] but by ending the split in third, they had to go through the Play-In stage.

The Play-In stage proved to be no challenge for the French team. Dominating over the group in a 6-0 record, they also made quick work of [[Iron Wolves]] in the knockout stage to make it to the main group stage of the event. Continuing their momentum, Karmine Corp again finished the group stage on a 5-1 record, initially ending in a tie with [[X7 Esports]], but they were able to beat them in the tiebreaker to get the 1st place seed. The team then started their run in the Knockout Stage against [[Unicorns_of_Love_Sexy_Edition|Unicorns of Love]], followed by a match against LFL counterparts [[Vitality.Bee]] in the semifinals. Although Karmine Corp initially fell behind 0-2 in the series, Karmine Corp was able to pull off the reverse sweep to place themselves in the Grand Finals and earned themselves a rematch against [[LDLC OL]]. The encounter this time went in favour of Karmine Corp as they beat LDLC 3-1 to earn their third European Masters title.

The following [[LFL/2022/Summer|Summer Split]] was a fall from grace for Karmine Corp, having their worst showing in the LFL thus far. Initially only placing sixth in the regular season with a 9-9 record, they were also defeated by [[GameWard]] in the first round of the playoffs, ending their split in 6th place. This also was the first time they missed out on EU Masters since they were in the LFL. Ending the year Karmine Corp then parted ways with [[113]] as he later joined [[Astralis]] to join the [[LEC]], and [[Rekkles]] leaving the organization as he made his return to [[Fnatic]]. Karmine Corp later also parted ways with [[Hantera]] as they then rebuilt for the 2023 season.

Starting 2023 Karmince Corp brought in Duncan "[[Skeanz]]" Marquet, Muhammed "[[Kaori]]" Şentürk, and Alexandru "[[whiteinn]]" Kolozsvari alongside new Head Coach Stefan "[[Nerroh]]" Pereira. This new roster failed to gain any momentum in the [[LFL/2023/Spring|Spring Split]], falling into 9th place early on in the regular season, and remaining there all remaining weeks. This also saw Karmince Corp fall out of playoff contention. A first since their appearance in the LFL.

Following their disastrous season, Karmine Corp was quick to rebuild the team for [[LFL/2023/Summer|Summer]]. Replacing the players they brought in earlier the year, and instead bringing back familiar faces in Jakub "[[Cinkrof]]" Rokicki and Raphaël "[[Targamas]]" Crabbé. Alongside this they brought in the 17-year-ol rookie Caliste "[[Caliste]]" Henry-Hennebert as their Botlaner.

With the newly assembled team, Karmine Corp returned to form in rapidly. Soaring to first place in the regular season on a 14-4 record. For their playoffs run they then faced off against [[BK ROG Esports]] twice in both their first round and the grand finals, both times convincingly beating the team. With these victories, they secured themselves their second LFL title and their fourth appearance at [[EMEA_Masters/2023/Summer|EU Masters]] (which had now been renamed to EMEA Masters).

This time in the Group Stage of [[EMEA_Masters/2023/Summer|EMEA Masters]] Karmine Corp was tested more. As they lost twice to the [[PG_Nationals/2023/Summer|Italian]] [[Macko Esports]], and being on a 1-1 record with [[Nativz]] from the [[NLC]] they had to play a tiebreaker match to make it out of the Group Stage. They managed to find their win and were then seeded against the [[İstanbul Wildcats]] in the Quarterfinals, who came off a dominant showing in their group on a 6-0 record. However, Karmine Corp was able to sweep the series in a clean 3-0, finding themselves against fellow [[LFL]] counterparts [[Team GO]] in the Semifinals. In a back-and-forth series, they managed to gain the upper hand in the latter half, securing a 3-2 victory to send them to the Grand Finals in Montpellier to face off against [[Movistar Riders]]. The series started off in the favour of Karmine Corp, as they quickly found a 2-0 lead against the Spanish team. However, the Movistar Riders were able to tie up the series, leaving it up to a final deciding game. Karmine Corp though, managed to come through and won the final game, claiming their fourth European Masters Title.

===Entrance into the LEC (2024)===
On October 18th, 2023 it was announced that Karmine Corp had acquired the [[LEC]] slot from [[Astralis]]. <ref name=kc_slot_article></ref>

